---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AgeCalculator from '@/components/tools/datetime/AgeCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="age-calculator">
  <AgeCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the age calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the date of birth. All dates from year 1 to year 9999 work, including dates before 1970.</li>
      <li>
        Leave <strong>Age at date</strong> on today for the current age, or change it to find the age on a
        specific day in the past or future.
      </li>
      <li>
        Turn on <strong>I know my time of birth</strong> to make the hour totals exact. The last day of the
        breakdown then rolls over at the minute of birth, not at midnight.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check the reference timezone. It sets what "today" is, which can change the answer by one day near
        midnight or for an age in another country.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How exact age is calculated</h2>
    <p>
      Age is not a division. Elapsed days divided by 365.25 gives a decimal that no form, school or immigration
      office accepts. This calculator uses the convention of all legal systems that define age: a calendar walk in
      three steps.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Count whole years from the date of birth to the reference date.</li>
      <li>From that anniversary, count whole calendar months.</li>
      <li>The days that remain are the day component.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Step two uses real month lengths, so 31 January to 1 March is "1 month, 1 day" in a leap year and also in a
      common year. The month component absorbs the difference. The totals below are a separate measurement. They
      count real elapsed time, so they include each leap day you have lived through.
    </p>

    <h3>People born on 29 February</h3>
    <p>
      A leap-day birthday has no anniversary in three years out of four. This calculator uses the most common
      civil convention: 28 February is the anniversary in common years, as in most databases and calendar
      libraries. Some jurisdictions use 1 March, so check the local rule if a legal deadline depends on it. Total
      days lived does not change.
    </p>

    <h3>Why the timezone matters</h3>
    <p>
      "How old am I" depends on today's date, and today's date depends on where you are. At 23:30 on Tuesday in
      Los Angeles it is already Wednesday in London, so the two answers can differ by a day. For the age that your
      birth certificate gives, set the reference timezone to the country of birth.
    </p>

    <h2>What each total actually measures</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Figure</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
            <th>Typical use</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Years, months, days</td>
            <td>Whole calendar units, counted in that order</td>
            <td>Forms, medical records, school entry, visas</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Total days</td>
            <td>Whole calendar days between the two dates</td>
            <td>Milestone birthdays such as the 10,000th day</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Total months</td>
            <td>Whole calendar months, so exactly 12 per year</td>
            <td>Infant development charts, subscription age</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Total hours and minutes</td>
            <td>Real elapsed time, including leap days and DST shifts</td>
            <td>Curiosity, birthday-party trivia, precise anniversaries</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Milestone birthdays worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>1,000 days: about 2 years and 9 months.</li>
      <li>10,000 days: about 27 years and 4 months.</li>
      <li>1 billion seconds: 31 years and a little more than 251 days.</li>
      <li>20,000 days: about 54 years and 9 months.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To find the exact day of a milestone, move the <strong>Age at date</strong> field forward and watch the
      total-days figure.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      A date of birth is very useful to an identity thief. This page has no analytics, no forms and no network
      requests. The calculation runs in your browser, the value is not stored, and closing the tab erases it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
